findings.body:
The filter predicate was flipped from `state.globalEnabled` to `!state.globalEnabled`:

::diff{file="packages/features/feature-opt-in/services/FeatureOptInService.ts" lines="207-207"}

Now the user settings page (`/settings/my-account/features`) shows exactly the features that are switched off globally, and hides every feature that's actually live. The doc comment just above still says "Only returns features that are … globally enabled". `listFeaturesForTeam` at line 242 still filters on `result.globalEnabled`, so user and team results now disagree.

No unit test covers `listFeaturesForUser`, which is why this got through.

::suggestion{file="packages/features/feature-opt-in/services/FeatureOptInService.ts" lines="207-207"}
    return featureIds.map((featureId) => resolvedStates[featureId]).filter((state) => state.globalEnabled);

The condition changed from `state === "inherit"` to `state !== "inherit"`, but the branch bodies stayed the same:

::diff{file="packages/features/feature-opt-in/services/FeatureOptInService.ts" lines="264-274"}

Here's what happens now:
- **`enabled` / `disabled`** calls the repository with no `assignedBy`. `FeaturesRepository.setUserFeatureState` then upserts `UserFeatures` with `assignedBy: undefined`. That column is a required `String` in `schema.prisma` (line 1717), so Prisma throws and **users can never opt in or out**.
- **`inherit`** reads `input.assignedBy` (undefined) and sends `assignedBy: "user-undefined"`. The repository just deletes the row, so this case happens to work.

`setTeamFeatureState` right below still uses `state === "inherit"`. The new unit test `should set user feature state when scope allows` expects `assignedBy: "user-2"`, so it should fail on this code.

